INT. SMALL CLAIMS COURT, STRIKE ZONE LANES ANNEX - DAY

A bowling alley, daytime. A folding table stands at the end of lane four. The pins are still set. A hand-lettered sign: "COURT IN SESSION. NO SHOES, NO SERVICE."

JUDGE FAIRCLOTH, sixties, in a black robe over a bowling shirt, sits on a high stool. ELSPETH MORROW, forties, the plaintiff, stands with a binder. MR. ABERNATHY, seventies, the defendant, sits in a plastic chair clutching a trophy.

                    FAIRCLOTH
          Ms. Morrow, you allege Mr. Abernathy broke your window with a bowling ball.

                    ELSPETH
          I allege it, Your Honor. Lane six, left-hand hook, two a.m.

                    ABERNATHY
          It wasn't two. It was one-forty.

                    FAIRCLOTH
          Mr. Abernathy, you'll have your turn.

                    ABERNATHY
          (muttering)
          One-forty. I wrote it down.

                    ELSPETH
          The ball came through my bathroom window, Your Honor.

                    FAIRCLOTH
          From the alley?

                    ELSPETH
          From the parking lot. He was bowling in the parking lot.

Abernathy grips the trophy. Faircloth leans down from the stool and gestures with his gavel to the empty lanes beyond, as if the gallery were full.

                    FAIRCLOTH
          (to the room)
          Let the record show we have no jury, no clerk, and no coffee.

He taps the gavel. It bounces off the table and rolls under lane four.
